
cat — Display entire file
=========================
cat filename
cat /var/log/app.log
cat -n filename  --> Display with line numbers:


more — Display file page by page
================================
more filename
more application.log

Note: enter space for next page, enter q to quit


less — View large files page by page
====================================
less filename

Note:
Space → next page
b → previous page
j -> one line forward or downward arrow key
k -> one line backward or upward arrow key
/word → search
n → next search result
q → quit


head — Display beginning of a file
==================================
head filename   --> first 10 lines
head -20 filename   --> first 20 lines


tail — Display end of a file
============================
tail filename     --> Last 10 lines
tail -20 filename  --> Last 20 lines
tail -f application.log   --> Monitor a file continuously, Very useful for real-time application logs
Note: Press Ctrl+C to stop.


nl — Display file with line numbers
===================================
nl test.txt


sed — Display specific lines
============================
sed -n '50p' filename    --> Display line 50
sed -n '50,100p' filename --> Display lines 50–100


awk — Display selected columns
==============================
awk '{print $1, $2}' filename    --> For a space-separated file, Display first and second columns


grep — Display lines matching a pattern
=======================================
grep "ERROR" application.log
grep -i "error" application.log   --> Case-insensitive
grep -n "ERROR" application.log   --> With line numbers







